approaches the variance boundary. And ten Hove, Jorgensen & van der Ark
(2020) found percentile (not HPD) intervals give nominal coverage at small
rater counts. The HPDI is offered for comparison, not as a strict upgrade,
and no coverage is claimed for it. Only the HPDI needs the posterior draws,
so \code{posterior_summary = "hpdi"} requires \code{ci_method = "posterior"}. The
other interval methods already report a percentile interval.}
}
\value{
\code{icc()} returns an \code{icc} object: a list with the estimate table,
variance components, design, engine, interval settings, sample sizes, the
fitted model, and the call.

The methods documented on this page return:
\itemize{
\item \code{tidy.icc()}: a tibble with one row per estimated coefficient. Its
columns are \code{index}, \code{type}, \code{level}, \code{sf_index}, \code{estimate},
\code{std.error}, \code{conf.low}, \code{conf.high}, \code{conf.level}, and \code{method}. An
\code{occasions} column joins them when the design has within-cell replicates,
inserted after \code{index}, so the list above is not a column order.
\item \code{glance.icc()}: a one-row tibble of model-level summaries:
the sample sizes, the design flags, the effective rater counts, the
variance components, and the engine and interval settings.
\item \code{format.icc()}: a character vector holding the printed report, one line per
element.
\item \code{print.icc()}: the \code{icc} object invisibly, having emitted that report.
\item \code{summary.icc()}: the \code{icc} object invisibly, having emitted the report
followed by the interpretive notes.
\item \code{autoplot.icc()}: a \code{ggplot} object holding the coefficient forest plot,
or the variance-component decomposition when \code{what = "components"}.
\item \code{plot.icc()}: the \code{icc} object invisibly, having drawn that same plot.
}

\code{tidy.icc()} and \code{glance.icc()} implement the \link[generics:tidy]{tidy()} and
\link[generics:glance]{glance()} generics.
}
\description{
Estimates interrater-reliability intraclass correlation coefficients (ICCs)
from a fitted linear mixed model, rather than from classical ANOVA mean
squares. \code{icc()} computes the two-way \strong{absolute-agreement} (\verb{ICC(A,*)}) or
\strong{consistency} (\verb{ICC(C,*)}) coefficients of McGraw & Wong (1996). It reports
them for a single rater (\verb{ICC(*,1)}) or the mean of \code{k} raters (\verb{ICC(*,k)}),
treating the raters as a random sample (Case 2) or as fixed (Case 3).
}
\section{Which ICC is this, and when should you use it?}{

Three choices pin down the coefficient:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Agreement vs. consistency} (\code{type}). \strong{Absolute agreement} treats
systematic differences between raters (the rater main effect,
\eqn{\sigma^2_r}) as error: use it when the actual value matters and raters
must agree on the number (clinical scores, measurements). \strong{Consistency}
ignores a constant per-rater offset: use it when only relative standing
matters. A large gap between the two signals big systematic differences in
rater level, which is a rating-procedure problem worth fixing.
\item \strong{Single vs. average} (\code{unit}). \strong{\verb{ICC(*,1)}} is the reliability of a
\emph{single} rater, and \strong{\verb{ICC(*,k)}} is the reliability of the \emph{mean} of your \code{k}
raters. Report \verb{ICC(*,k)} when the averaged score is what you will use.
\item \strong{Random vs. fixed raters} (\code{raters}). \strong{Random} treats your raters as a
sample you wish to generalize beyond, and is the recommended default for
interrater reliability. \strong{Fixed} treats them as the only raters of interest
and forgoes generalization. It is fit separately, with raters as fixed
effects, so on balanced data it matches the random point estimate but on
incomplete data it genuinely differs. \code{icc()} warns when you choose it.
Fixed-rater
consistency is the classic Shrout & Fleiss \code{ICC(3,1)}.
}
}

\section{Estimand}{

With a single rating per subject-by-rater cell, the subject-by-rater
interaction and pure error are not separately identified. In that case only
their sum, the residual variance \eqn{\sigma^2_{res}}, is estimable. Absolute agreement counts
the rater main effect \eqn{\sigma^2_r} as error. Consistency drops it.
\deqn{ICC(A,1) = \sigma^2_s / (\sigma^2_s + \sigma^2_r + \sigma^2_{res})}
\deqn{ICC(A,k) = \sigma^2_s / (\sigma^2_s + (\sigma^2_r + \sigma^2_{res}) / k)}
\deqn{ICC(C,1) = \sigma^2_s / (\sigma^2_s + \sigma^2_{res})}
\deqn{ICC(C,k) = \sigma^2_s / (\sigma^2_s + \sigma^2_{res} / k)}
Here \eqn{\sigma^2_s} is the subject (signal) variance and \code{k} is the number
of raters.
}

\section{Multilevel designs (subject vs. cluster level)}{

When subjects are nested in higher-level clusters (pupils in classrooms,
patients in clinics), single-level ICCs conflate the levels and are biased
(ten Hove et al. 2022). Supplying \code{cluster} fits the five-component Design-1
model
\deqn{score \sim 1 + (1|cluster) + (1|cluster{:}subject) + (1|rater) + (1|cluster{:}rater)}
and reports two distinct reliabilities. The \strong{subject level} (within-cluster)
asks how reliably raters distinguish subjects \emph{within} a cluster: its signal is
the between-subject-within-cluster variance and cluster variance drops out. The
\strong{cluster level} (between-cluster) asks how reliably raters distinguish cluster
means: its signal is the between-cluster variance and the rater-disagreement
error is the cluster-by-rater term. Choose the level that matches the decision
you will make (about a subject, or about a cluster). The agreement/consistency
and single/average choices above apply at each level.

\code{level = "conflated"} reports the \strong{biased single-level ICC} you would get by
\emph{ignoring} the clustering (ten Hove et al. 2022, Eq. 14). Between- and
within-cluster subject variance are both counted as signal, and the
rater-related terms as error. It is offered only as a \strong{diagnostic contrast},
to quantify how much the nesting distorts reliability, and is never a
recommended coefficient. \code{print()} flags it as such.
It is the \strong{flat two-way ICC} read off the multilevel
fit, so it comes in both \code{type} forms: absolute agreement (Eq. 14) and
\strong{consistency} (which drops the rater main-effect variance, McGraw & Wong 1996).
It needs a crossed (Design 1) random-rater design and works on both balanced and
\strong{incomplete} data (same \code{k_eff} divisor). Because it reads the cluster-by-rater
variance, it needs raters that bridge clusters. Without bridging, the
conflated level is dropped, like the cluster level. Request it alongside the
correct levels, e.g. \code{level = c("subject", "cluster", "conflated")}.

The design is \strong{inferred from the data} (ten Hove et al. 2022, Table 2). If
raters are crossed with clusters (each rater rates in every cluster) the
five-component model above is used (Design 1). Because the design is read from
the rater \strong{labels}, a rater label that appears in more than one cluster is
taken to be the \emph{same} rater (crossed). If your raters are cluster-specific but
share labels, give them cluster-unique labels or declare
\code{design = "nested_in_clusters"}. An example is "rater 1"/"rater 2" reused in
every cluster, which is a nested design. Otherwise the design is treated as
crossed and \code{icc()} prints a one-time note of that assumption.
If raters are \strong{nested in
clusters} (each cluster has its own raters, Design 2) a four-component model is
fit, with the rater variance carried by the nested rater-within-cluster term. If
raters are \strong{nested in subjects} (each subject has its own raters, Design 3) the
rater variance is confounded into the residual, giving a three-component
multilevel \emph{one-way} model that reports agreement-only \code{ICC(1)}/\code{ICC(k)}. Both
nested designs define only the \strong{subject} level, because a cluster-level ICC
needs raters crossed with clusters, so \code{level} is restricted to \code{"subject"}
for them.
Mixed patterns (some raters crossed, some nested) are not a supported design and
raise an error. The \strong{crossed} design (Design 1) additionally supports
\strong{incomplete} data, meaning subjects rated by different, overlapping rater
subsets (missing cells). On such data it computes the subject-level ICCs by
REML, with the averaging divisor set to the effective number of ratings per
subject (\code{k_eff}, the harmonic mean). That is exactly what the single-level
incomplete two-way ICC does. Identifiability is
checked first: each cluster's subject-by-rater layout must be connected, and for
absolute agreement raters must bridge clusters (otherwise the design is really
rater-nested). When missing cells make the crossed-vs-nested pattern ambiguous,
declare it with \code{design} (above). On incomplete data the \strong{cluster} level is
reported, when raters bridge clusters, as both the single-rater \code{ICC(c,1)} and
the averaged \code{ICC(c,k)}. The average divides the cluster error by the
effective number of raters behind each cluster's observed (cells-pooled) mean.
That is the inverse-Simpson harmonic \code{k_c^eff}, reported as \code{k_c_eff}, and
equal to the rater count on complete data. A rater-balanced cluster mean would
have a different (higher) effective count. This averaged cluster \code{ICC(c,k)} on
incomplete data ships for every random-rater engine: \code{glmmTMB}, \code{lme4}, and
\code{brms}. The divisor is applied to the posterior draws' variance components
exactly as for the frequentist fits.
\strong{Fixed raters}
(\code{raters = "fixed"}) are supported for the crossed design at the \strong{subject}
level on both balanced and \strong{incomplete} data. For that design and level, the
rater main effect becomes the finite-population variance of the observed raters
(McGraw & Wong Case 3A). So on balanced data consistency is identical to the random-rater case, and
absolute agreement differs only by that term. On incomplete data both types
differ from random, and the finite-population variance is read from the ragged
rater-contrast fit.
\strong{Nested (Design 2) fixed raters} are likewise supported at the \strong{subject}
level on both balanced and \strong{incomplete} data. There the finite-population
rater variance is formed \strong{per cluster}, from each cluster's own raters, and
averaged over clusters. On ragged data each cluster uses its own effective
rater count. The fixed-rater \strong{cluster}
level is supported for the crossed (Design 1) design on \strong{balanced, complete}
data. Its signal is \eqn{\sigma^2_c}, and its agreement error is the
finite-population \eqn{\theta^2_r} plus the cluster-by-rater term
\eqn{\sigma^2_{cr}}. On balanced data it equals the
random-rater cluster-level ICC. The Bayesian (\code{engine = "brms"}) fixed-rater
\strong{cluster} level is likewise supported for the crossed (Design 1) design on
balanced, complete data, and the Bayesian incomplete/ragged fixed-rater \strong{nested}
(Design 2) subject level is supported too. Incomplete/unbalanced fixed-rater
cluster-level estimation and Design-3 fixed raters (nested in subjects, with
no separable rater effect) remain for later milestones.
}

\section{Within-cell replicates}{

When a subject-by-rater cell is rated \strong{more than once} (within-cell
replicates), \code{icc()} fits the two-way random model \strong{with a subject-by-rater
interaction}: \code{score ~ 1 + (1|subject) + (1|rater) + (1|subject:rater)}.
That splits the single-rating residual into the \strong{interaction}
\eqn{\sigma^2_{sr}} and \strong{pure error} \eqn{\sigma^2_e} (rating noise). The
interaction asks whether a rater systematically rates a subject high or low,
in stable disagreement. Both are reported. The
single-occasion ICCs are unchanged in value from a one-rating-per-cell
analysis, because a single rating's error still includes the interaction. The
components are no longer confounded, though. And \code{occasions = "average"}
reports the reliability of the mean of the replicates, which reduces
\eqn{\sigma^2_e} but not \eqn{\sigma^2_{sr}}. With \code{raters = "fixed"} the
rater main effect becomes the finite-population \eqn{\theta^2_r} (McGraw &
Wong Case 3A, fit as
\code{score ~ 1 + rater + (1|subject) + (1|subject:rater)}). On balanced, complete
data \eqn{\theta^2_r = \sigma^2_r}, so fixed reproduces the random-rater
coefficients.
\strong{Multilevel} replicated designs add a \code{(1|cluster:subject:rater)} term (crossed
Design 1 and nested Design 2), splitting the highest-order residual at the subject
level. \strong{Ragged} (unequal per-cell counts or missing cells) two-way random data
fits the \strong{single-occasion} family directly, the replicate analogue of an
incomplete design. The occasion-averaged coefficient on ragged data is not yet
supported, because there is no single effective occasion count to average
over. One-way
replicates, fixed or multilevel ragged replicates, and \code{d_study()} projection off a
replicate fit are planned for later milestones.
}

\section{Confidence intervals}{

Intervals are Monte-Carlo: parameters are drawn from the fitted covariance on
the model's internal (log) scale and back-transformed, so the interval is
boundary-aware near the common zero-rater-variance case where the delta method
fails. Pass \code{seed} for a reproducible interval.

\strong{Coverage caveat: skewed or heavy-tailed subject effects.} The
simulation draws parameters from a normal approximation to the fitted
covariance, and that approximation degrades when the \emph{subject} effects
themselves are strongly skewed or heavy-tailed. A one-way simulation study
measured the default's coverage well below its nominal level across such
data. It was worst at chi-square(1) subject effects with a true ICC of 0.6,
50 subjects and 5 raters, where intervals that were produced at all covered
0.6725 of the time. At 5 raters per subject, coverage falls as the subject
count rises once the true ICC is moderate or high. Fewer raters is not a
refuge: in every cell where both were measured, 2 raters covered worse than
5 did. But a larger share of those runs abort instead of reporting an
interval, and this caveat is about what does get reported. Near-normal and
uniform subject effects under-covered only in cells where many runs
aborted. Among cells that almost always report an interval, they showed no
shortfall. Held-out cells at lognormal and Laplace subject effects
under-covered at that same 50-subject, 5-rater geometry, covering 0.825 and
0.84, while their 20-subject, 3-rater cells were near nominal.

This is not repaired by switching to a closed form. In every cell where the
default under-covered without also aborting often, the balanced one-way
opt-ins \code{"searle"} and \code{"burch"} under-covered too, and usually by more
(see \code{ci_method}). The remaining methods were not run on that study, so
nothing here recommends one. Treat an interval on visibly skewed or
heavy-tailed subject effects as optimistic, and prefer reporting the
variance components alongside it.
}

\section{The \code{"npbootstrap"} interval (one-way)}{
For \code{unit = "average"} (the ICC(k), reliability of the mean of the \emph{k} ratings)
the transformed bootstrap-\emph{t} interval is the exact monotone \strong{Spearman-Brown}
image of the single-rating ICC(1) interval. The map
\code{g(rho) = k_eff*rho / (1 + (k_eff-1)*rho)} is applied to the two ICC(1)
endpoints, with \code{k_eff} the effective number of ratings per subject (the
harmonic mean, \verb{= k} on balanced data). Because that map is strictly
increasing on the attainable range, the ICC(k) interval's coverage is
\strong{identical to the ICC(1) interval's, by construction}. It is not a separate
approximation.

On \strong{unbalanced} data (unequal ratings per subject) the reducer uses the ANOVA
effective group size \code{n0 = (N - sum(n_i^2)/N) / (k - 1)} (Ohyama 2025) in the
\verb{log F} transform. It studentizes \code{log(SSA) - log(SSE)}, the pivot the
infinitesimal-jackknife SE is derived for (Ukoumunne et al. 2003, Appendix A),
which coincides with the balanced \verb{log F} pivot when subjects are equally rated.
The Spearman-Brown map stays well-defined unbalanced because \code{k_eff <= n0} for
every one-way design. So its pole \code{-1/(k_eff-1)} sits at or below the ICC(1)
support boundary \code{-1/(n0-1)} and never falls inside the interval. Coverage
inheritance therefore holds unbalanced exactly as it does balanced. A numeric
\code{unit} (D-study projection to \code{m} raters), by contrast, is balanced-only: a chosen
\code{m} may exceed \code{n0} and push the pole inside the support.

Following Ukoumunne et al. (2003, §5.2), the endpoints are \strong{not truncated} to
\verb{[0, 1]}. They are confined only to the estimator's own support, approaching
\code{-1/(n0-1)} from above for ICC(1), and unbounded below for ICC(k). So a
near-boundary lower endpoint can be negative, markedly so for ICC(k). Leaving
them untruncated is what makes the coverage faithful to the published method. On
unbalanced data the reported ICC(k) \code{std.error} (the spread of the resampled
ICC(k) values) can likewise be large near the boundary, where a resample close to
the pole inflates the untruncated ICC(k) scale. This is a faithful disclosure,
not an error, and the coverage-bearing endpoints are unaffected.

The reported \strong{point estimate} is the engine (REML) point, exactly as for every
other \code{ci_method}. \code{ci_method} selects the interval, not the estimator. At the
zero-between-variance boundary the point sits at, or numerically indistinguishable
from, \code{0}, while the untruncated interval
may extend below \code{0}. This is the normal picture for a boundary-respecting point
beside an honest interval, and it signals that the data are consistent with values
near and below zero.
}

\section{The classical \code{"searle"} and \code{"burch"} intervals (balanced one-way)}{
Both are deterministic closed forms from the one-way ANOVA. \code{"searle"} inverts the
exact-F pivot \code{F / (1 + n*lambda) ~ F(k-1, k(n-1))} (Searle 1971, Ch. 9 Table 9.14;
the McGraw & Wong 1996 Table 7 limits). It is exact under normality. \code{"burch"} builds
kurtosis-adjusted \code{log(1 + n*theta-hat)} limits (Burch 2011), so its width tracks
the data's tail weight rather than widening by construction. On the two grids
this package has measured that vary only the subject effect it came out
narrower than \code{"searle"} in nearly every cell.
How much narrower is conditional, and not in the direction one might guess.
\code{"burch"}'s width margin holds much the same up to a true ICC of 0.3 rather than shrinking as the true ICC rises
(on the larger grid; the smaller grid's margin does shrink across its levels).
The margin then collapses to near parity at a true ICC of 0.6, on the one grid reaching that value,
where every cell favouring \code{"searle"} sits.
And \code{"burch"}'s width margin
shrinks steadily as the subject count grows, measured at 5 raters.
Burch reports the reverse for symmetric heavy-tailed data with non-normal
errors, and a third grid now measures that case.
What \code{"burch"} does against \code{"searle"} depends on what the residual is
drawn from. The three grids now measure that:
the two grids that vary only the subject effect put it narrower
nearly everywhere, while the third, which draws the residual from
the same family as the subject effect, puts it wider at every
symmetric heavy-tailed family measured (a median width ratio of
1.2963 at t(5) with 100 subjects) and narrower at every lighter-tailed one,
the normal included.
Its robustness has a measured limit: on strongly skewed subject effects
\code{"burch"} under-covers about as badly as the default (see the coverage caveat
under Confidence intervals). Both share the conventions above. For both, the
\code{unit = "average"} (ICC(k))
interval is the same exact monotone \strong{Spearman-Brown} image of the ICC(1)
endpoints, so its coverage is identical by construction. Their endpoints are left
\strong{untruncated} on the estimator's own support, and the reported \strong{point} is the
engine (REML) point. Being closed forms they take no \code{mc_samples}, \code{boot_samples},
or \code{seed}, and report no \code{std.error} (there is no sampling distribution). Their
value is a finite, well-calibrated interval on the near-zero-ICC boundary where the
Monte-Carlo default aborts.
}

